WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:10.880
On a very symbolic day, Christmas 1991, the
flag of the Soviet Union was pulled down from

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.619
the Kremlin's flagpole once and for all.

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:23.420
It was the end of an era that had lasted almost
70 years.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.550
That evening pretty much every tv news broadcasted
live the resignation speech of the last secretary

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:36.809
of the Soviet Communist Party: Mikhail Gorbachev.

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:42.270
The first international reaction was that
of the US President George Bush Senior who,

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:49.079
for three times, described the event as America's
definitive victory over communism.

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:56.019
However, the question that many asked themselves
at the time was: how had it been possible

00:00:56.019 --> 00:00:57.250
to get to that point?

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.649
The collapse of the Soviet Union was a sequence
of causes.

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:05.650
Internal and external, old and rotten, all
typical causes of a crippled system, magnified

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.520
by a sequence of major errors.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.320
To discuss the collapse we must go back a
few years earlier, to November 1982.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.650
Gorbachev wasn't yet the General Secretary
of the Communist Party.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.360
That role was held by Jurij Andropov.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.760
To be honest, the idea of "restructuring"
the Soviet Union did not start with Gorbachev.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.430
But with Andropov.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:31.630
Many in Russia were saying: if only Andropov
had lived a little longer, the USSR would

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:32.630
have survived.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:37.520
Well, when he was appointed by Brezhnev as
his successor, Andropov was aware that he

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.800
was inheriting a Soviet Union full of problems.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.050
By 1982, the Soviets were at war with Afghanistan,
détente with the West had failed, and Reagan

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.830
- an anti-communist champion - had ascended
to the White House.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:56.050
In Eastern Europe, things weren't any better:
in Poland, workers were demanding a decrease

00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:00.960
in food prices and, with the help of dissident
intellectuals, they had founded a movement

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.250
of revolt, Solidarity, which in the early
eighties already had millions of members.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:11.260
The problems, however, were not "fresh" ones:
the USSR had many open questions since its

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:17.690
foundation, and one of these can be ascribed
to one single word: historical instability.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:22.640
Since the time of the Revolution, Socialism
in Russia claimed to be able to modernize

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.310
a nation that, unlike Western Europe, was
still trapped in economic and political chaos.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:33.350
It was like playing a strategy video game:
pick the USSR and you get a starting handicap

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.430
compared to other Western nations.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.590
This starting handicap generated an immature
and unripe condition for the communists in

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:46.110
power, which made them force the stages of
a modernization process that had taken centuries

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.110
in the West.

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:51.760
To put it in even more basic terms that will
piss off any chef living in Italy, they wanted

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.220
to make pasta by boiling it in still lukewarm
water.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.680
For some, the Soviet Union was created on
unstable foundations: an economy torn apart

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:07.110
by the incapacity of the imperial government,
working classes that were really still underdeveloped

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.160
and the absence of really decisive cultural
movements.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.400
This condition of intrinsic failings of the
system, would persist throughout the whole

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.400
Soviet history.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:20.800
Sure, with Stalin, heavy industrialization
and increased centralization brought changes

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.150
in the economy, they were a decisive impulse
to transform the Soviet Union into a nation

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:32.030
no longer backward, the second most industrial
nation in the world, yet the latent problems

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:37.230
didn't disappear, they only festered: the
majority population were no longer peasants

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.230
but workers.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.380
Goodbye, Kulaks.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:44.560
And with both Stalin and his successors Krushev
and Brezhnev, the economy was dictated by

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.400
a single key word: intensity...

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.700
when in fact the Soviets needed the opposite,
extensivity.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.500
I’m not sure if this word exists in English.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.360
To beat the West, the USSR adopted the mantra
of quantity over quality.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:00.670
Although far from the American model, the
Soviet one would always be based on a "Fordist"

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:06.250
conception, let's say, where almost everyone
ended up in a factory working to contribute

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.110
to the huge production of materials, first
for war and then for civilian use.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:16.070
And it remained so, even when in the rest
of the Western world, in the 80s, the arrival

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:20.060
of a new technology began to be foreseen:
information technology.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.960
As promoters of a socialist model, the big
problem of the Soviets was being immediately

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.820
subjected to a very strong political isolation
and economic embargoes by that advanced part

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.430
of the world, the West.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:37.960
The Soviet Union was indeed autonomous, but
ended up being a separate universe on its

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:38.960
own.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.220
In the 80's, Andropov was the first to realize
that in order to modernize the Soviet economy

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.650
there were a need for money and knowledge
that was abundant in the West.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.590
This idea obviously didn’t diminish the
value and the quality of many Soviet scientists,

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:57.600
but it rather diminished the immobility that
for decades marked the Communist Party.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:04.240
As Andropov confessed in 1981 to Erich Mielke,
head of the Stasi - the East German secret

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.640
service: "The Americans seem to have unlimited
money".

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:13.820
Andropov had a point: NATO, Japan and the
Arab states of the Gulf contributed to financing

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:19.590
the American debt and its state funds, including
military spending; on the other hand, the

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:24.830
Soviet Union had a whole series of socialist
states that depended exclusively on subsidies

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.100
from Mother Moscow.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.410
The isolation of the socialist countries had
contributed to making it clear to everyone

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:35.280
that it was capitalism that was dictating
the rules of the game, and the fact that with

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.930
détente in the 1970s the Soviet Union had
taken up the idea of opening a dialogue with

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.220
the United States, and thus knocking on the
door of those who controlled the capitalist

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.710
market, was - to use the words of Eric Hobsbawm
- the beginning of the end.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:56.139
The first signs of weakness in the Soviet
Union appeared in 1979, when the international

00:05:56.139 --> 00:05:59.360
markets were hit by a very serious oil crisis.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.210
The wave fell with all its power on the Soviet
budgets that relied heavily on the export

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:06.020
of gas and crude oil.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.960
The USSR was in fact the first oil producing
nation, with more than eleven million barrels

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:11.960
per day).

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:18.770
But it was another energy crisis that messed
up the game, not that of 79 but that of 73.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:23.949
In that year, a West without oil decided to
renew itself technically and scientifically

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:28.340
so to make itself less dependent on crude
oil exporting countries and at the same time

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.330
find new ways of earning money; Russia, instead,
was satisfied to export oil to Western countries

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.120
and had no incentive to innovate itself.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:41.430
At a meeting in 1982 Andropov denounced a
very disturbing fact: the Soviets were importing

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:45.449
more and more food such as grain and meat
from foreign countries.

00:06:45.449 --> 00:06:53.820
Andropov said "I don't wanna alarm you," "but,"
he continued, "in the last few years we have

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:55.720
wasted billions of rubles.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:02.041
Strong words, strong words from a strange
man, though in reality he was right, because

00:07:02.041 --> 00:07:07.699
instead of using oil revenues to invest them
in technology and knowledge for development,

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:12.820
the Soviet Union used them to do what it should
not do: import food - that it could produce

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:17.270
very well by itself - and give subsidies to
its puppet states.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.740
The United States did nothing but say thanks.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:25.910
With the oil crisis in 79, the Russians found
themselves with no money left to pay for food

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:27.100
imports.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.341
At that point what did they do?

00:07:29.341 --> 00:07:32.400
They started borrowing money from Western
foreigners themselves.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.520
The first to experience the mechanism of debt
on their own skin were the countries of Eastern

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.240
Europe, the Baltics but especially Romania,
Poland and Hungary, where the need to repay

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:48.870
foreign debt increased social unrest, which,
in turn, led to popular uprisings that slowly

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:52.110
brought Eastern Europe away from Soviet control.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:57.570
The first countries to incur debts with the
International Monetary Fund were Yugoslavia,

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:01.840
which was no longer aligned with Moscow, and
then it was the turn of the friends in the

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.210
Warsaw Pact.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:09.539
Already in 1970 Moscow found itself surrounded
by satellite states with negative budgets

00:08:09.539 --> 00:08:12.009
and with pockets full of debts with the West.

00:08:12.009 --> 00:08:18.240
When a group of dissidents created Solidarnosc
in Poland in 1980, Andropov became aware of

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:25.479
one thing: the inability to intervene militarily
as USSR had done with Czechoslovakia in 1968.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:31.199
Moreover, the underlying problem wasn't tangible,
the problem was the absence of money.

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:36.889
As pointed out by historian Vladislav Zubok:
Poland had accumulated $27 billion in debt

00:08:36.889 --> 00:08:41.990
to Western banks at very high interest rates
and, for its part, the Soviets did not have

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:45.180
the capital to repay the debts of the Poles.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:50.730
The Russians were well aware of the implications
of this: their own satellite states had become

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:57.120
the weak links that risked blowing up COMECON,
the economic alliance within the Union republics.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:02.270
In an investigation by Carl Bernstein, a journalist
known for having contributed to the unraveling

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:07.200
of the Watergate scandal in America, it was
reported that Solidarnosc was nothing more

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:13.490
than a tool in the hands of the United States
and financed by the italian bank Banco Ambrosiano

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.529
– in connection with the Vatican - to undermine
the USSR from within.

00:09:17.529 --> 00:09:23.370
Nothing new on the Western front: Actually
the exact quote from the book is “All quiet

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.440
on the Western Front”.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:28.520
it's just that in Italian we say that way
and I translated it literally.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:29.920
Mamma mia.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.060
the Vatican hoped to take root not only in
the very religious Poland but also in the

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:39.940
rest of Eastern Europe; and the United States,
well, needless to say: they wanted to win

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:40.940
the race.

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:45.680
As Bernestein reported, the U.S. embassy in
the Polish capital of Warsaw had in fact become

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.660
the CIA headquarters on the other side of
the Iron Curtain.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:55.140
Undoubtedly, in Poland there were already
discontents, the citizens were very suffering,

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:59.810
otherwise it would have been impossible for
the western secret services to create such

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.940
massive riots out of nothing.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:09.510
The use secret services was common on both
sides; nuclear weapons forced to act in the

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:10.510
background.

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:15.200
And speaking of nuclear armaments, there are
those who say that the arms race and the military

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.470
spending budget contributed to the Soviet
imbalance.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:23.510
According to Mark Harrison, professor and
expert in Economic History, it was not military

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:26.050
spending that endangered the Soviet economy.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.970
In terms of numbers, the active personnel
in the Soviet Union in the 80's amounted to

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:37.490
more than 5 million and 12% of the GDP was
dedicated to the budget for the war industry;

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:43.230
in comparison, the maximum that the United
States had done was in 1963, when they allocated

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.450
9% of GDP.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:48.160
The United States, having a greater number
of allies at its disposal and therefore more

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:53.550
resources to draw on, could tolerate with
more ease this competition which, at the end

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:58.640
was aimed at frantically bleeding the Soviet
coffers, something that the Secretary of State

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:01.120
George Schulz himself would later admit.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:06.370
In the 80's, when Reagan threated the USSR
with the installation of the Euro missiles,

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:11.830
the Soviets had to intensify their efforts
by running after advanced technologies and

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:17.430
creating an arsenal of more than 6000 nuclear
warheads, a huge strain for the weak Soviet

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:20.870
economy that generated irreparable imbalances.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:25.330
In fact, since Stalin took the leadership,
the Soviet economy had been based on a state

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:31.519
of permanent war and neurosis, and this had
pushed the Russians to prefer heavy industry

00:11:31.519 --> 00:11:35.930
- first of all war and industrial machineries
- at the expense of consumer goods.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:41.370
And as if that weren't enough, despite Kosygin's
unsuccessful attempts to reform the economy

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:46.870
in the 1960s, Soviet planning - which was
centralized and state-based - had serious

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:52.890
cracks in it: false and inaccurate information,
inflated production costs, excess materials

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:59.089
produced without accounting for the real quantities
needed, low labor productivity, low rhythms,

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:01.590
absenteeism, and poorly exploited labor.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.160
All this only widened the gap with the overly
competitive capitalist world.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:11.839
If you were a Soviet worker, whether you worked
hard 10 hours a day or not, that salary always

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:12.839
came.

00:12:12.839 --> 00:12:14.610
So why make an unnecessary effort?

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:18.760
The same happened with the salaries of all
the professions (from doctors to engineers)

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:25.370
which, since Krushov in the 60's, had been
fixed at a predetermined ceiling beyond which

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:26.540
they rarely went.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:30.980
It is logical that without the possibility
of economic progress, one does not have the

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:32.720
incentive to get better.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:37.329
Practical examples such as these generated
over the decades a loss of confidence in socialism

00:12:37.329 --> 00:12:38.339
by the Soviets.

00:12:38.339 --> 00:12:43.700
In the 1970s and 1980s in Russia, as reported
by historian Zaslavsky, workers were passive,

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:51.050
indifferent, lacking ideals and driven by
purely consumerist behavior, a pessimism also

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:53.610
intensified by the failure of the invasion
of Afghanistan.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:57.850
Society had entered a labyrinth of economic
and social crises.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:03.170
The same Soviet companies, subsidized by the
State, did not aim at technological innovation

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:05.920
and investment, because nothing would change.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:11.680
In a nation where little food, few medicines,
few clothes or household appliances were produced,

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:14.600
things were bound to get jammed up.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:19.370
With the crisis of 1979, the distribution
of essential supplies became more and more

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:20.370
complex.

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:25.320
When, under Gorbachev at the end of the 1980s,
food prices conformed to those of the Western

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:30.899
free market, the damage was complete and empty
shelves in supermarkets became the norm in

00:13:30.899 --> 00:13:31.899
Russia.

00:13:31.899 --> 00:13:36.100
Another internal issue that had been building
up since the Fifties in the Soviet Union then

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:38.399
deepened the crisis of essential goods.

00:13:38.399 --> 00:13:39.399
The Double Economy.

00:13:39.399 --> 00:13:44.610
Today we would call it "the black economy",
a shadow economy, parallel to the state economy

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:49.839
and composed of transactions that went under
the scanner of the Soviet bureaucracy.

00:13:49.839 --> 00:13:54.160
In short, the Double Economy deprived the
State of revenue, making it flow into the

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.160
black market.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:00.430
Goods such as cars, for which there were very
long waiting lists, were an excellent opportunity

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:03.290
on which many bureaucrats speculated, by reselling
them on the black market at very high prices.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:08.520
This involved any item: furniture, spare parts,
clothes and even houses construction.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:12.700
The black market meant that, in addition to
money, materials and labor were being stolen

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:13.700
from the state.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:18.850
Over time, all over the USSR - especially
the areas most remote from Moscow - an underworld

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:24.649
of businessmen - whom the historian Zubok
calls pseudo-capitalists - had formed.

00:14:24.649 --> 00:14:28.130
These were real entrepreneurs with money,
materials and workers.

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:33.690
They contolled all of this under the table
to raise more money and earn more than the

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:36.120
standard salary offered by the State.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:41.480
Konstantin Simis in his book "The Secret World
of Soviet Capitalism" states: "Under the socialist

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.480
surface lies a network of private companies.

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:50.649
Thousands of them produce sweaters, shoes,
sunglasses, cassettes with Western pop music,

00:14:50.649 --> 00:14:52.060
handbags and many other goods.

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:56.690
The owners are both small businessmen and
multimillionaire family clans holding dozens

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:58.089
of companies."

00:14:58.089 --> 00:15:03.649
When Gorbachev came to power in '85, no politician
or bureaucrat was exempt from this under-the-table

00:15:03.649 --> 00:15:04.649
trafficking.

00:15:04.649 --> 00:15:09.580
The underground economy, which in the 60's
was equivalent to 3% of the national economy,

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:13.620
in the 90's in the Soviet Union reached 13%.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:18.149
Prostitution and drugs also contributed to
a large part of the Soviet underground economy.

00:15:18.149 --> 00:15:22.610
It was in this illegality that corruption
and the Mafias found very fertile ground.

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:23.610
Already in 1974, among the highest Party officials,
the Moscow Mafia had profitable business relations.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:27.940
With the fierce privatizations in the early
90's the Russian Mafia found a real treasure;

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:33.290
that was the period when organized crime became
the true master of the streets, the dispenser

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:39.279
of death and violence for millions of Russians
who fell into the Abìss of drug addiction.

00:15:39.279 --> 00:15:44.139
. And unlike his predecessors who at least
tried to hide things, Gorbachev instilled

00:15:44.139 --> 00:15:49.660
the idea of a lost war; he was the first to
say in his speeches that capitalism won, and

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.810
communism didn't.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:52.500
Attitude of a true leader.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:58.470
Many blamed the Soviet nomenklatura and bureaucracy
who enjoyed special privileges and lived as

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.890
if they were a class of their own.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:05.290
State inertia, excessive bureaucratization
and the indifference of Party members had

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:09.090
created a huge gap between citizens and politics.

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:13.589
The politicians themselves were so old that
they were referred to as a "gerontocracy,"

00:16:13.589 --> 00:16:17.740
a government of elders bloated by nepotism
and cronyism.

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:22.780
And clearly when the old guys are in power,
all gathered into one party, they only care

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:25.100
about keeping that power until they die.

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:26.540
What about improving the economy?

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:28.779
Pff who damn cares?

00:16:28.779 --> 00:16:33.930
In the Soviet Union there were two nomenklatures:
the political-administrative one, which permeated

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:38.790
the single Party in Moscow, and the economic
one made up of military, intellectuals and

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:39.830
business managers.

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:42.760
That the two sides were separated was not
a good thing.

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.810
Because when the central state - hence the
bureaucrats in Moscow - increasingly weakened

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:51.589
by the scarcity of money and consumer goods
began to lose relevance and influence in the

00:16:51.589 --> 00:16:56.290
country, the power passed more and more into
the hands of the economic nomenklatura, that

00:16:56.290 --> 00:17:02.139
is those who actually held the real resources
of the country: directors who managed industries,

00:17:02.139 --> 00:17:07.370
estates and mines, the same ones who later,
with the fierce sell-off of the best national

00:17:07.370 --> 00:17:09.839
companies, would turn into multi-billionaire
oligarchs.

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:15.399
In the liberal years of Gorbachev, the "economic
nomenklatura" chose to play with intelligence,

00:17:15.399 --> 00:17:20.679
opening private companies and joint ventures
with Western partners, by giving them valuable

00:17:20.679 --> 00:17:24.260
information previously kept by party bureaucrats.

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:28.979
And that wasn’t all: they also offered most
of their resources that the Soviet Union enjoyed

00:17:28.979 --> 00:17:31.460
in abundance, such as oil and gas.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:36.090
Everything officially started with the famous
Perestrojka, literally "the reconstruction".

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:43.590
Perestroika proved one thing: that the breakup
of the USSR, before Gorbachev, was not a foregone

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:44.590
conclusion.

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:49.899
Gorbachev - who is still alive and is 90 years
old - has yet to be fully analysed by historians.

00:17:49.899 --> 00:18:15.629
For the moment I will always remember him
for his awkward commercial [...] Essentially

00:18:15.629 --> 00:18:20.639
with that appearance which was paid 1 million
dollars, Gorby implicitly admitted in front

00:18:20.639 --> 00:18:25.859
of everyone that consumerism was superior
to the ideology of its own party.

00:18:25.859 --> 00:18:31.369
The funny thing was that Gorbachev always
invoked Lenin's name in his rallies, he considered

00:18:31.369 --> 00:18:36.330
him a mentor, but perestroika, initially presented
as something revolutionary, ended up becoming

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.440
the opposite, the dismantling of the whole
system.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:43.169
The slogan was "acceleration", recovering
the gap with the West.

00:18:43.169 --> 00:18:48.210
But in order to do so, Gorbachev implemented
his reforms on the basis of two conditions

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:53.330
that would have got Lenin's hair fall - at
least the remaining ones: de-statalization,

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:57.259
i'm not sure if this word exists in English,
and privatization.

00:18:57.259 --> 00:19:01.799
According to some historians, Gorbachev acted
consciously, he wanted to dismantle the Soviet

00:19:01.799 --> 00:19:07.260
system piece by piece, to embrace the dogmas
of economic liberalism, deceived by the benefits

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.900
of economic exchange with the West.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:14.279
For others, Gorbachev was simply an idiot
who had no idea how to lead a country.

00:19:14.279 --> 00:19:19.149
He instead admitted that he was convinced
that it wasn’t possible to reform the country

00:19:19.149 --> 00:19:25.299
without first dismantling it, making it transparent
and free from the maniacal control of state

00:19:25.299 --> 00:19:28.549
censorship, hence the concept of Glasnost'.

00:19:28.549 --> 00:19:33.570
Glasnost was in fact the sounding board of
the disaster of perestroika, because the media

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:38.389
began to talk about those problems in Russia
that were under the eyes of all but no one

00:19:38.389 --> 00:19:42.850
could mention: alcoholism, lack of food and
crumbling housing.

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:46.989
And speaking of alcohol: the drinking habit
of Russians is well known, to the point of

00:19:46.989 --> 00:19:48.940
being a social plague.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:53.850
Gorbachev thought it was time to eradicate
the problem by implementing an anti-alcohol

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:54.850
policy.

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:59.659
The catch was, however, that alcohol taxes
guaranteed the state a sum of about six hundred

00:19:59.659 --> 00:20:01.450
and sixty-six million dollars.

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:06.619
However, the Gosplan, the State Planning Committee
- under pressure from Gorbachev himself - cut

00:20:06.619 --> 00:20:11.690
off that source of revenue, raising drink
prices and prosecuting those caught in a...

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.809
well, miserable state.

00:20:14.809 --> 00:20:20.220
Nonetheless, the ban didn't take into account
a detail: the black market.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:24.370
The vodka that became a luxury good ended
up again "m3rachiulusly" resold through the

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:25.830
back door.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:31.379
The financial disaster was quick to come:
sales of vodka dropped from 54 billion rubles

00:20:31.379 --> 00:20:35.399
in 1984 to 11 billion in 1986.

00:20:35.399 --> 00:20:42.169
In Gorbachev's mind, however, there were no
issues: this is it, in 5 years we'll heal

00:20:42.169 --> 00:20:43.169
USSR.

00:20:43.169 --> 00:20:48.080
Gorbachev reasoned by taking as a model what
happened in the 30s: at that time, the USSR

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:53.200
had begun to modernize thanks to the construction
of industries and plants erected by Western

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:58.859
companies that, on commission, had also trained
Soviet engineers and workers from scratch.

00:20:58.859 --> 00:21:04.070
One example above all was the American architect
Albert Kahn, who joined the Kremlin's consulting

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:08.479
team for the construction of more than five
hundred industrial plants; another example

00:21:08.479 --> 00:21:13.820
was the General Electric, which oversaw the
construction of the hydroelectric power plant

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:15.570
on the Dniepr River.

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:20.580
Instead, Gorbachev's mistake was to channel
money into existing state enterprises, not

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:21.950
to create new ones.

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:25.580
The old leaders were conservative, unwilling
to innovate.

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:29.639
And that's why much of the equipment that
had been purchased in the West was never used

00:21:29.639 --> 00:21:30.750
in the old plants.

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:31.750
Brilliant.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:36.029
Anyway, whatever plans Gorbachev and his team
of economists had for the long term...

00:21:36.029 --> 00:21:40.419
well, the Chernobyl nuclear disaster in April
86 erased them altogether.

00:21:40.419 --> 00:21:45.320
The flight of hundreds of thousands of people
from the surrounding areas evoked scenarios

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:46.320
from World War II.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:51.249
According to the head of the economic department
Nikolai Ryzhkov, the cost of the Chernobyl

00:21:51.249 --> 00:21:55.299
disaster for the first month alone was 8 billion
rubles.

00:21:55.299 --> 00:22:00.549
To paraphrase Ryzhkov, Chernobyl delivered
a devastating blow to the Soviet economy.

00:22:00.549 --> 00:22:06.419
People began to whisper that it was the " tainted
leader", Gorbachev, to bring bad luck... he

00:22:06.419 --> 00:22:10.970
was called in that disdainful way because
of the visible spot on his forehead.

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:15.379
Gorbachev did not make himself very popular,
as he kept the entire Russian population in

00:22:15.379 --> 00:22:17.869
the dark about the disaster for many days.

00:22:17.869 --> 00:22:23.140
Apart from Chernobyl, Gorbachev went on with
his Perestroika, and what were the results?

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:24.140
Terrible.

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:28.279
Perestroika liberalized overnight a Soviet
economy that was planned from the beginning,

00:22:28.279 --> 00:22:29.549
it was a devastating blow.

00:22:29.549 --> 00:22:34.830
First, government officials approved the creation
of "free" prices, no longer controlled and

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:36.169
set by the state.

00:22:36.169 --> 00:22:39.190
Only 4% of the citizens approved this maneuver.

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:40.190
The result?

00:22:40.190 --> 00:22:46.389
A race to the top of basic necessities and
a hyperinflation that in 1992 would have touched

00:22:46.389 --> 00:22:52.769
peaks of Two Thousand Six Hundred %. Money
was rationed and in its place a sort of "consumer

00:22:52.769 --> 00:22:57.289
card" was introduced, they were sheets of
paper worth various amounts of rubles, with

00:22:57.289 --> 00:23:00.809
which citizens could make purchases without
using cash money.

00:23:00.809 --> 00:23:05.399
When they found a piece of bread after hours
standing in line, Russians could count themselves

00:23:05.399 --> 00:23:10.659
lucky if they could buy a half-pound loaf
of bread for 6 dollars, compared to an average

00:23:10.659 --> 00:23:12.499
monthly salary of fifty dollars.

00:23:12.499 --> 00:23:17.109
Although the Soviet standard of living until
then had always been at least a third lower

00:23:17.109 --> 00:23:22.159
than that of Westerners, citizens noticed
the difference with respect to previous times:

00:23:22.159 --> 00:23:26.899
crime, especially the mafia, was lower and
there was a general sense of greater security.

00:23:26.899 --> 00:23:29.739
Whether this was real or not is another story.

00:23:29.739 --> 00:23:34.859
With Perestroika there was a free-for-all
for those who wanted to do business with large

00:23:34.859 --> 00:23:39.940
foreign multinationals, an incentive from
which stemmed a whole series of speculation

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:45.399
and fake sales, like the ones of the strategic
Soviet mining companies at symbolic prices.

00:23:45.399 --> 00:23:50.049
On top of that, there was the Issuance of
new currencies for all the republics of the

00:23:50.049 --> 00:23:51.049
Union.

00:23:51.049 --> 00:23:54.080
This increased the confusion of internal transactions
and trade.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:59.429
In addition, the so-called "property law"
of 1990, which removed the state from ownership

00:23:59.429 --> 00:24:05.250
of key industries, generated 2 million unemployed
nationwide, a figure never seen before.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:09.619
And as we know, an unemployed population use
more and more alcohol and drugs, to say the

00:24:09.619 --> 00:24:10.619
least.

00:24:10.619 --> 00:24:15.539
By 98, the Russian economy, in the hands of
mobsters and foreign entrepreneurs, would

00:24:15.539 --> 00:24:19.039
have been almost completely halved from 1990
figures, going from a GDP of five hundred

00:24:19.039 --> 00:24:20.320
and ten billion to a GDP of two hundred and
seventy.

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:22.830
Food prices had doubled, and wages were less
than half.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:28.509
While, typhoid, cholera and other diseases
had reached epidemic proportions in the country.

00:24:28.509 --> 00:24:32.649
It was as if the entire nation had suddenly
gone back 80 years.

00:24:32.649 --> 00:24:39.200
Some, such as historian Stephen Cohen, have
called this achievement the result of a particular

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.299
phenomenon, Gangster Capitalism.

00:24:41.299 --> 00:24:46.929
The opening up to international markets also
encouraged the arrival of private banks, which

00:24:46.929 --> 00:24:49.330
replaced the monopoly of the State Bank.

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:55.330
Soviet debt to Western banks jumped from twenty
seven point two billion dollars in 1985 to

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:59.409
forty billion $ in 1986.

00:24:59.409 --> 00:25:03.999
Wanting to reform things too quickly also
invested the political front.

00:25:03.999 --> 00:25:07.969
Gorbachev knew that the Party - and its members
- were the operational core.

00:25:07.969 --> 00:25:13.159
If he wanted to change the Union structure,
the only way was to strip Party members of

00:25:13.159 --> 00:25:16.519
their various Departments and deprive them
of their powers.

00:25:16.519 --> 00:25:22.139
With a series of reforms Gorbachev did just
that, he took away the Party's decision-making

00:25:22.139 --> 00:25:23.139
monopoly.

00:25:23.139 --> 00:25:27.399
In doing so, however, it was like beheading
the State, it was a political suicide, in

00:25:27.399 --> 00:25:28.399
all respects.

00:25:28.399 --> 00:25:31.820
Without a head - the State - the body (the
Soviet Union) suddenly stopped working, a

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:34.559
bit like unplugging a device.

00:25:34.559 --> 00:25:39.580
At that point, all it took was a little push,
an international event of great impact that

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:43.039
could maybe break up the entire Eastern bloc.

00:25:43.039 --> 00:25:56.099
Oh, yeah, how silly, this already happened
in 1989: --- With the symbolic fall of the

00:25:56.099 --> 00:26:00.899
Berlin Wall, like a domino, all the Soviet
republics, starting with the Baltic ones,

00:26:00.899 --> 00:26:05.779
broke away one after the other in the wake
of the independence euphoria that horrified

00:26:05.779 --> 00:26:07.249
the Russian nationalists.

00:26:07.249 --> 00:26:11.840
And nationalism was the final card, the ace
of spades that would close the game.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:17.109
The first to realize that the rules of that
game were changing, was Boris Yeltsin.

00:26:17.109 --> 00:26:22.649
Like an old fox, Yeltsin exploited the frustrations
of the most radical communists and some of

00:26:22.649 --> 00:26:27.479
the members of the secret service to break
away from Gorbachev's wing, dress up as a

00:26:27.479 --> 00:26:34.580
populist leader, go into opposition, and support
a coup against Gorbachev in 1991 in the capital

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:35.580
Moscow.

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:40.149
The images of him getting on the tank would
go around the world, angering the most dissatisfied

00:26:40.149 --> 00:26:43.299
people who were fed up with Gorbachev's policies.

00:26:43.299 --> 00:26:47.259
The coup failed immediately, because obviously
there was no longer a state.

00:26:47.259 --> 00:26:53.179
It must be said that 70% of the population
wanted to maintain the Soviet Union, the referendum

00:26:53.179 --> 00:26:57.409
on the preservation of the USSR in 1991 clearly
demonstrated this.

00:26:57.409 --> 00:27:00.090
But these were not Yeltsin's plans.

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:04.919
Yeltsin had all the popular support to be
able to obscure Gorbachev, take away his remaining

00:27:04.919 --> 00:27:08.559
consensus and force him to resign at the end
of August.

00:27:08.559 --> 00:27:12.739
In November of the same year Yeltsin would
issue a decree with which to make illegal

00:27:12.739 --> 00:27:18.179
any activity of the Communist Party and dissolve
once and for all the Soviet State.

00:27:18.179 --> 00:27:25.129
On December 11, 1991 only two of the 15 Soviet
republics were still part of the Union: Russia

00:27:25.129 --> 00:27:26.129
and Kazakhstan.

00:27:26.129 --> 00:27:29.950
The former left the Union the following day,
on December 12.

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:37.789
That was the end of the USSR and the beginning
of the Russian Federation.

00:27:37.789 --> 00:27:45.429
[gentlemen titanic].

00:27:45.429 --> 00:27:50.169
It’s not over yet.

00:27:50.169 --> 00:27:53.149
With the exit of Russia, Kazakhstan became
the Soviet Union.

00:27:53.149 --> 00:27:54.149
Yep.

00:27:54.149 --> 00:28:00.750
For 4 days, until it declared itself an independent
republic on December 16, 1991, Kazakhstan

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:02.729
was basically the Soviet Union.

00:28:02.729 --> 00:28:08.299
Oh great Kazakhstan, thank you, you created
a nightmare that even Lenin would never have

00:28:08.299 --> 00:28:09.339
dared to dream.

00:28:09.339 --> 00:28:12.229
I've been wanting to make this video for almost
a year, I tried 3 times previously but I always

00:28:12.229 --> 00:28:13.229
gave up.

00:28:13.229 --> 00:28:14.450
Summarizing the reasons for the collapse of
the Soviet Union in a 30 minutes video was

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:15.450
unthinkable.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:16.450
Yet here we are.

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:17.450
Thanks to all of you for listening to me until
the end.

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:18.450
See you in the next episode.

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:19.450
Ciao!

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:20.450
On a very symbolic day, Christmas 1991, the
flag of the Soviet Union was pulled down from

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:21.450
the Kremlin's flagpole once and for all.

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:22.450
It was the end of an era that had lasted almost
70 years.

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:23.450
That evening pretty much every tv news broadcasted
live the resignation speech of the last secretary

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:24.450
of the Soviet Communist Party: Mikhail Gorbachev.

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:25.450
The first international reaction was that
of the US President George Bush Senior who,

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:26.450
for three times, described the event as America's
definitive victory over communism.

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:27.450
However, the question that many asked themselves
at the time was: how had it been possible

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:28.450
to get to that point?

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:29.450
The collapse of the Soviet Union was a sequence
of causes.

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:30.450
Internal and external, old and rotten, all
typical causes of a crippled system, magnified

00:28:30.450 --> 00:28:31.450
by a sequence of major errors.

00:28:31.450 --> 00:28:32.450
To discuss the collapse we must go back a
few years earlier, to November 1982.

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:33.450
Gorbachev wasn't yet the General Secretary
of the Communist Party.

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:34.450
That role was held by Jurij Andropov.

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:35.450
To be honest, the idea of "restructuring"
the Soviet Union did not start with Gorbachev.

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:36.450
But with Andropov.

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:37.450
Many in Russia were saying: if only Andropov
had lived a little longer, the USSR would

00:28:37.450 --> 00:28:38.450
have survived.

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:39.450
Well, when he was appointed by Brezhnev as
his successor, Andropov was aware that he

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:40.450
was inheriting a Soviet Union full of problems.

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:41.450
By 1982, the Soviets were at war with Afghanistan,
détente with the West had failed, and Reagan

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:42.450
- an anti-communist champion - had ascended
to the White House.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:43.450
In Eastern Europe, things weren't any better:
in Poland, workers were demanding a decrease

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:44.450
in food prices and, with the help of dissident
intellectuals, they had founded a movement

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:45.450
of revolt, Solidarity, which in the early
eighties already had millions of members.

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:46.450
The problems, however, were not "fresh" ones:
the USSR had many open questions since its

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:47.450
foundation, and one of these can be ascribed
to one single word: historical instability.

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:48.450
Since the time of the Revolution, Socialism
in Russia claimed to be able to modernize

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:49.450
a nation that, unlike Western Europe, was
still trapped in economic and political chaos.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:50.450
It was like playing a strategy video game:
pick the USSR and you get a starting handicap

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:51.450
compared to other Western nations.

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:52.450
This starting handicap generated an immature
and unripe condition for the communists in

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:53.450
power, which made them force the stages of
a modernization process that had taken centuries

00:28:53.450 --> 00:28:54.450
in the West.

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:55.450
To put it in even more basic terms that will
piss off any chef living in Italy, they wanted

00:28:55.450 --> 00:28:56.450
to make pasta by boiling it in still lukewarm
water.

00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:57.450
For some, the Soviet Union was created on
unstable foundations: an economy torn apart

00:28:57.450 --> 00:28:58.450
by the incapacity of the imperial government,
working classes that were really still underdeveloped

00:28:58.450 --> 00:28:59.450
and the absence of really decisive cultural
movements.

00:28:59.450 --> 00:29:00.450
This condition of intrinsic failings of the
system, would persist throughout the whole

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:01.450
Soviet history.

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:02.450
Sure, with Stalin, heavy industrialization
and increased centralization brought changes

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:03.450
in the economy, they were a decisive impulse
to transform the Soviet Union into a nation

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:04.450
no longer backward, the second most industrial
nation in the world, yet the latent problems

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:05.450
didn't disappear, they only festered: the
majority population were no longer peasants

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:06.450
but workers.

00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:07.450
Goodbye, Kulaks.

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:08.450
And with both Stalin and his successors Krushev
and Brezhnev, the economy was dictated by

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:09.450
a single key word: intensity...

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:10.450
when in fact the Soviets needed the opposite,
extensivity.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:11.450
I’m not sure if this word exists in English.

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:12.450
To beat the West, the USSR adopted the mantra
of quantity over quality.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:13.450
Although far from the American model, the
Soviet one would always be based on a "Fordist"

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:14.450
conception, let's say, where almost everyone
ended up in a factory working to contribute

00:29:14.450 --> 00:29:15.450
to the huge production of materials, first
for war and then for civilian use.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:16.450
And it remained so, even when in the rest
of the Western world, in the 80s, the arrival

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:17.450
of a new technology began to be foreseen:
information technology.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:18.450
As promoters of a socialist model, the big
problem of the Soviets was being immediately

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:19.450
subjected to a very strong political isolation
and economic embargoes by that advanced part

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:20.450
of the world, the West.

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:21.450
The Soviet Union was indeed autonomous, but
ended up being a separate universe on its

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:22.450
own.

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:23.450
In the 80's, Andropov was the first to realize
that in order to modernize the Soviet economy

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:24.450
there were a need for money and knowledge
that was abundant in the West.

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:25.450
This idea obviously didn’t diminish the
value and the quality of many Soviet scientists,

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:26.450
but it rather diminished the immobility that
for decades marked the Communist Party.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:27.450
As Andropov confessed in 1981 to Erich Mielke,
head of the Stasi - the East German secret

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:28.450
service: "The Americans seem to have unlimited
money".

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:29.450
Andropov had a point: NATO, Japan and the
Arab states of the Gulf contributed to financing

00:29:29.450 --> 00:29:30.450
the American debt and its state funds, including
military spending; on the other hand, the

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:31.450
Soviet Union had a whole series of socialist
states that depended exclusively on subsidies

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:32.450
from Mother Moscow.

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:33.450
The isolation of the socialist countries had
contributed to making it clear to everyone

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:34.450
that it was capitalism that was dictating
the rules of the game, and the fact that with

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:35.450
détente in the 1970s the Soviet Union had
taken up the idea of opening a dialogue with

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:36.450
the United States, and thus knocking on the
door of those who controlled the capitalist

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:37.450
market, was - to use the words of Eric Hobsbawm
- the beginning of the end.

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:38.450
The first signs of weakness in the Soviet
Union appeared in 1979, when the international

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:39.450
markets were hit by a very serious oil crisis.

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:40.450
The wave fell with all its power on the Soviet
budgets that relied heavily on the export

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:41.450
of gas and crude oil.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:42.450
The USSR was in fact the first oil producing
nation, with more than eleven million barrels

00:29:42.450 --> 00:29:43.450
per day).

00:29:43.450 --> 00:29:44.450
But it was another energy crisis that messed
up the game, not that of 79 but that of 73.

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:45.450
In that year, a West without oil decided to
renew itself technically and scientifically

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:46.450
so to make itself less dependent on crude
oil exporting countries and at the same time

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:47.450
find new ways of earning money; Russia, instead,
was satisfied to export oil to Western countries

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:48.450
and had no incentive to innovate itself.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:49.450
At a meeting in 1982 Andropov denounced a
very disturbing fact: the Soviets were importing

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:50.450
more and more food such as grain and meat
from foreign countries.

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:51.450
Andropov said "I don't wanna alarm you," "but,"
he continued, "in the last few years we have

00:29:51.450 --> 00:29:52.450
wasted billions of rubles.

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:53.450
Strong words, strong words from a strange
man, though in reality he was right, because

00:29:53.450 --> 00:29:54.450
instead of using oil revenues to invest them
in technology and knowledge for development,

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:55.450
the Soviet Union used them to do what it should
not do: import food - that it could produce

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:56.450
very well by itself - and give subsidies to
its puppet states.

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:57.450
The United States did nothing but say thanks.

00:29:57.450 --> 00:29:58.450
With the oil crisis in 79, the Russians found
themselves with no money left to pay for food

00:29:58.450 --> 00:29:59.450
imports.

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:00.450
At that point what did they do?

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:01.450
They started borrowing money from Western
foreigners themselves.

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:02.450
The first to experience the mechanism of debt
on their own skin were the countries of Eastern

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:03.450
Europe, the Baltics but especially Romania,
Poland and Hungary, where the need to repay

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:04.450
foreign debt increased social unrest, which,
in turn, led to popular uprisings that slowly

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:05.450
brought Eastern Europe away from Soviet control.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:06.450
The first countries to incur debts with the
International Monetary Fund were Yugoslavia,

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:07.450
which was no longer aligned with Moscow, and
then it was the turn of the friends in the

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:08.450
Warsaw Pact.

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:09.450
Already in 1970 Moscow found itself surrounded
by satellite states with negative budgets

00:30:09.450 --> 00:30:10.450
and with pockets full of debts with the West.

00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:11.450
When a group of dissidents created Solidarnosc
in Poland in 1980, Andropov became aware of

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:12.450
one thing: the inability to intervene militarily
as USSR had done with Czechoslovakia in 1968.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:13.450
Moreover, the underlying problem wasn't tangible,
the problem was the absence of money.

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:14.450
As pointed out by historian Vladislav Zubok:
Poland had accumulated $27 billion in debt

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:15.450
to Western banks at very high interest rates
and, for its part, the Soviets did not have

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:16.450
the capital to repay the debts of the Poles.

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:17.450
The Russians were well aware of the implications
of this: their own satellite states had become

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:18.450
the weak links that risked blowing up COMECON,
the economic alliance within the Union republics.

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:19.450
In an investigation by Carl Bernstein, a journalist
known for having contributed to the unraveling

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:20.450
of the Watergate scandal in America, it was
reported that Solidarnosc was nothing more

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:21.450
than a tool in the hands of the United States
and financed by the italian bank Banco Ambrosiano

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:22.450
– in connection with the Vatican - to undermine
the USSR from within.

00:30:22.450 --> 00:30:23.450
Nothing new on the Western front: Actually
the exact quote from the book is “All quiet

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:24.450
on the Western Front”.

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:25.450
it's just that in Italian we say that way
and I translated it literally.

00:30:25.450 --> 00:30:26.450
Mamma mia.

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:27.450
the Vatican hoped to take root not only in
the very religious Poland but also in the

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:28.450
rest of Eastern Europe; and the United States,
well, needless to say: they wanted to win

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:29.450
the race.

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:30.450
As Bernestein reported, the U.S. embassy in
the Polish capital of Warsaw had in fact become

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:31.450
the CIA headquarters on the other side of
the Iron Curtain.

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:32.450
Undoubtedly, in Poland there were already
discontents, the citizens were very suffering,

00:30:32.450 --> 00:30:33.450
otherwise it would have been impossible for
the western secret services to create such

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:34.450
massive riots out of nothing.

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:35.450
The use secret services was common on both
sides; nuclear weapons forced to act in the

00:30:35.450 --> 00:30:36.450
background.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:37.450
And speaking of nuclear armaments, there are
those who say that the arms race and the military

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:38.450
spending budget contributed to the Soviet
imbalance.

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:39.450
According to Mark Harrison, professor and
expert in Economic History, it was not military

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:40.450
spending that endangered the Soviet economy.

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:41.450
In terms of numbers, the active personnel
in the Soviet Union in the 80's amounted to

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:42.450
more than 5 million and 12% of the GDP was
dedicated to the budget for the war industry;

00:30:42.450 --> 00:30:43.450
in comparison, the maximum that the United
States had done was in 1963, when they allocated

00:30:43.450 --> 00:30:44.450
9% of GDP.

00:30:44.450 --> 00:30:45.450
The United States, having a greater number
of allies at its disposal and therefore more

00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:46.450
resources to draw on, could tolerate with
more ease this competition which, at the end

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:47.450
was aimed at frantically bleeding the Soviet
coffers, something that the Secretary of State

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:48.450
George Schulz himself would later admit.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:49.450
In the 80's, when Reagan threated the USSR
with the installation of the Euro missiles,

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:50.450
the Soviets had to intensify their efforts
by running after advanced technologies and

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:51.450
creating an arsenal of more than 6000 nuclear
warheads, a huge strain for the weak Soviet

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:52.450
economy that generated irreparable imbalances.

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:53.450
In fact, since Stalin took the leadership,
the Soviet economy had been based on a state

00:30:53.450 --> 00:30:54.450
of permanent war and neurosis, and this had
pushed the Russians to prefer heavy industry

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:55.450
- first of all war and industrial machineries
- at the expense of consumer goods.

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:56.450
And as if that weren't enough, despite Kosygin's
unsuccessful attempts to reform the economy

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:57.450
in the 1960s, Soviet planning - which was
centralized and state-based - had serious

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:58.450
cracks in it: false and inaccurate information,
inflated production costs, excess materials

00:30:58.450 --> 00:30:59.450
produced without accounting for the real quantities
needed, low labor productivity, low rhythms,

00:30:59.450 --> 00:31:00.450
absenteeism, and poorly exploited labor.

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:01.450
All this only widened the gap with the overly
competitive capitalist world.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:02.450
If you were a Soviet worker, whether you worked
hard 10 hours a day or not, that salary always

00:31:02.450 --> 00:31:03.450
came.

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:04.450
So why make an unnecessary effort?

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:05.450
The same happened with the salaries of all
the professions (from doctors to engineers)

00:31:05.450 --> 00:31:06.450
which, since Krushov in the 60's, had been
fixed at a predetermined ceiling beyond which

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:07.450
they rarely went.

00:31:07.450 --> 00:31:08.450
It is logical that without the possibility
of economic progress, one does not have the

00:31:08.450 --> 00:31:09.450
incentive to get better.

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:10.450
Practical examples such as these generated
over the decades a loss of confidence in socialism

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:11.450
by the Soviets.

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:12.450
In the 1970s and 1980s in Russia, as reported
by historian Zaslavsky, workers were passive,

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:13.450
indifferent, lacking ideals and driven by
purely consumerist behavior, a pessimism also

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:14.450
intensified by the failure of the invasion
of Afghanistan.

00:31:14.450 --> 00:31:15.450
Society had entered a labyrinth of economic
and social crises.

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:16.450
The same Soviet companies, subsidized by the
State, did not aim at technological innovation

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:17.450
and investment, because nothing would change.

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:18.450
In a nation where little food, few medicines,
few clothes or household appliances were produced,

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:19.450
things were bound to get jammed up.

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:20.450
With the crisis of 1979, the distribution
of essential supplies became more and more

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:21.450
complex.

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:22.450
When, under Gorbachev at the end of the 1980s,
food prices conformed to those of the Western

00:31:22.450 --> 00:31:23.450
free market, the damage was complete and empty
shelves in supermarkets became the norm in

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:24.450
Russia.

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:25.450
Another internal issue that had been building
up since the Fifties in the Soviet Union then

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:26.450
deepened the crisis of essential goods.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:27.450
The Double Economy.

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:28.450
Today we would call it "the black economy",
a shadow economy, parallel to the state economy

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:29.450
and composed of transactions that went under
the scanner of the Soviet bureaucracy.

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:30.450
In short, the Double Economy deprived the
State of revenue, making it flow into the

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:31.450
black market.

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:32.450
Goods such as cars, for which there were very
long waiting lists, were an excellent opportunity

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:33.450
on which many bureaucrats speculated, by reselling
them on the black market at very high prices.

00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:34.450
This involved any item: furniture, spare parts,
clothes and even houses construction.

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:35.450
The black market meant that, in addition to
money, materials and labor were being stolen

00:31:35.450 --> 00:31:36.450
from the state.

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:37.450
Over time, all over the USSR - especially
the areas most remote from Moscow - an underworld

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:38.450
of businessmen - whom the historian Zubok
calls pseudo-capitalists - had formed.

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:39.450
These were real entrepreneurs with money,
materials and workers.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:40.450
They contolled all of this under the table
to raise more money and earn more than the

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:41.450
standard salary offered by the State.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:42.450
Konstantin Simis in his book "The Secret World
of Soviet Capitalism" states: "Under the socialist

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:43.450
surface lies a network of private companies.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:44.450
Thousands of them produce sweaters, shoes,
sunglasses, cassettes with Western pop music,

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:45.450
handbags and many other goods.

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:46.450
The owners are both small businessmen and
multimillionaire family clans holding dozens

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:47.450
of companies."

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:48.450
When Gorbachev came to power in '85, no politician
or bureaucrat was exempt from this under-the-table

00:31:48.450 --> 00:31:49.450
trafficking.

00:31:49.450 --> 00:31:50.450
The underground economy, which in the 60's
was equivalent to 3% of the national economy,

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:51.450
in the 90's in the Soviet Union reached 13%.

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:52.450
Prostitution and drugs also contributed to
a large part of the Soviet underground economy.

00:31:52.450 --> 00:31:53.450
It was in this illegality that corruption
and the Mafias found very fertile ground.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:54.450
Already in 1974, among the highest Party officials,
the Moscow Mafia had profitable business relations.

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:55.450
With the fierce privatizations in the early
90's the Russian Mafia found a real treasure;

00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:56.450
that was the period when organized crime became
the true master of the streets, the dispenser

00:31:56.450 --> 00:31:57.450
of death and violence for millions of Russians
who fell into the Abìss of drug addiction.

00:31:57.450 --> 00:31:58.450
. And unlike his predecessors who at least
tried to hide things, Gorbachev instilled

00:31:58.450 --> 00:31:59.450
the idea of a lost war; he was the first to
say in his speeches that capitalism won, and

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:00.450
communism didn't.

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:01.450
Attitude of a true leader.

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:02.450
Many blamed the Soviet nomenklatura and bureaucracy
who enjoyed special privileges and lived as

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:03.450
if they were a class of their own.

00:32:03.450 --> 00:32:04.450
State inertia, excessive bureaucratization
and the indifference of Party members had

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:05.450
created a huge gap between citizens and politics.

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:06.450
The politicians themselves were so old that
they were referred to as a "gerontocracy,"

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:07.450
a government of elders bloated by nepotism
and cronyism.

00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:08.450
And clearly when the old guys are in power,
all gathered into one party, they only care

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:09.450
about keeping that power until they die.

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:10.450
What about improving the economy?

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:11.450
Pff who damn cares?

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:12.450
In the Soviet Union there were two nomenklatures:
the political-administrative one, which permeated

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:13.450
the single Party in Moscow, and the economic
one made up of military, intellectuals and

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:14.450
business managers.

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:15.450
That the two sides were separated was not
a good thing.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:16.450
Because when the central state - hence the
bureaucrats in Moscow - increasingly weakened

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:17.450
by the scarcity of money and consumer goods
began to lose relevance and influence in the

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:18.450
country, the power passed more and more into
the hands of the economic nomenklatura, that

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:19.450
is those who actually held the real resources
of the country: directors who managed industries,

00:32:19.450 --> 00:32:20.450
estates and mines, the same ones who later,
with the fierce sell-off of the best national

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:21.450
companies, would turn into multi-billionaire
oligarchs.

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:22.450
In the liberal years of Gorbachev, the "economic
nomenklatura" chose to play with intelligence,

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:23.450
opening private companies and joint ventures
with Western partners, by giving them valuable

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:24.450
information previously kept by party bureaucrats.

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:25.450
And that wasn’t all: they also offered most
of their resources that the Soviet Union enjoyed

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:26.450
in abundance, such as oil and gas.

00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:27.450
Everything officially started with the famous
Perestrojka, literally "the reconstruction".

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:28.450
Perestroika proved one thing: that the breakup
of the USSR, before Gorbachev, was not a foregone

00:32:28.450 --> 00:32:29.450
conclusion.

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:30.450
Gorbachev - who is still alive and is 90 years
old - has yet to be fully analysed by historians.

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:31.450
For the moment I will always remember him
for his awkward commercial [...] Essentially

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:32.450
with that appearance which was paid 1 million
dollars, Gorby implicitly admitted in front

00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:33.450
of everyone that consumerism was superior
to the ideology of its own party.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:34.450
The funny thing was that Gorbachev always
invoked Lenin's name in his rallies, he considered

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:35.450
him a mentor, but perestroika, initially presented
as something revolutionary, ended up becoming

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:36.450
the opposite, the dismantling of the whole
system.

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:37.450
The slogan was "acceleration", recovering
the gap with the West.

00:32:37.450 --> 00:32:38.450
But in order to do so, Gorbachev implemented
his reforms on the basis of two conditions

00:32:38.450 --> 00:32:39.450
that would have got Lenin's hair fall - at
least the remaining ones: de-statalization,

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:40.450
i'm not sure if this word exists in English,
and privatization.

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:41.450
According to some historians, Gorbachev acted
consciously, he wanted to dismantle the Soviet

00:32:41.450 --> 00:32:42.450
system piece by piece, to embrace the dogmas
of economic liberalism, deceived by the benefits

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:43.450
of economic exchange with the West.

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:44.450
For others, Gorbachev was simply an idiot
who had no idea how to lead a country.

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:45.450
He instead admitted that he was convinced
that it wasn’t possible to reform the country

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:46.450
without first dismantling it, making it transparent
and free from the maniacal control of state

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:47.450
censorship, hence the concept of Glasnost'.

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:48.450
Glasnost was in fact the sounding board of
the disaster of perestroika, because the media

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:49.450
began to talk about those problems in Russia
that were under the eyes of all but no one

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:50.450
could mention: alcoholism, lack of food and
crumbling housing.

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:51.450
And speaking of alcohol: the drinking habit
of Russians is well known, to the point of

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:52.450
being a social plague.

00:32:52.450 --> 00:32:53.450
Gorbachev thought it was time to eradicate
the problem by implementing an anti-alcohol

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:54.450
policy.

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:55.450
The catch was, however, that alcohol taxes
guaranteed the state a sum of about six hundred

00:32:55.450 --> 00:32:56.450
and sixty-six million dollars.

00:32:56.450 --> 00:32:57.450
However, the Gosplan, the State Planning Committee
- under pressure from Gorbachev himself - cut

00:32:57.450 --> 00:32:58.450
off that source of revenue, raising drink
prices and prosecuting those caught in a...

00:32:58.450 --> 00:32:59.450
well, miserable state.

00:32:59.450 --> 00:33:00.450
Nonetheless, the ban didn't take into account
a detail: the black market.

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:01.450
The vodka that became a luxury good ended
up again "m3rachiulusly" resold through the

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:02.450
back door.

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:03.450
The financial disaster was quick to come:
sales of vodka dropped from 54 billion rubles

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:04.450
in 1984 to 11 billion in 1986.

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:05.450
In Gorbachev's mind, however, there were no
issues: this is it, in 5 years we'll heal

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:06.450
USSR.

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:07.450
Gorbachev reasoned by taking as a model what
happened in the 30s: at that time, the USSR

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:08.450
had begun to modernize thanks to the construction
of industries and plants erected by Western

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:09.450
companies that, on commission, had also trained
Soviet engineers and workers from scratch.

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:10.450
One example above all was the American architect
Albert Kahn, who joined the Kremlin's consulting

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:11.450
team for the construction of more than five
hundred industrial plants; another example

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:12.450
was the General Electric, which oversaw the
construction of the hydroelectric power plant

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:13.450
on the Dniepr River.

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:14.450
Instead, Gorbachev's mistake was to channel
money into existing state enterprises, not

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:15.450
to create new ones.

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:16.450
The old leaders were conservative, unwilling
to innovate.

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:17.450
And that's why much of the equipment that
had been purchased in the West was never used

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:18.450
in the old plants.

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:19.450
Brilliant.

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:20.450
Anyway, whatever plans Gorbachev and his team
of economists had for the long term...

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:21.450
well, the Chernobyl nuclear disaster in April
86 erased them altogether.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:22.450
The flight of hundreds of thousands of people
from the surrounding areas evoked scenarios

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:23.450
from World War II.

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:24.450
According to the head of the economic department
Nikolai Ryzhkov, the cost of the Chernobyl

00:33:24.450 --> 00:33:25.450
disaster for the first month alone was 8 billion
rubles.

00:33:25.450 --> 00:33:26.450
To paraphrase Ryzhkov, Chernobyl delivered
a devastating blow to the Soviet economy.

00:33:26.450 --> 00:33:27.450
People began to whisper that it was the " tainted
leader", Gorbachev, to bring bad luck... he

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:28.450
was called in that disdainful way because
of the visible spot on his forehead.

00:33:28.450 --> 00:33:29.450
Gorbachev did not make himself very popular,
as he kept the entire Russian population in

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:30.450
the dark about the disaster for many days.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:31.450
Apart from Chernobyl, Gorbachev went on with
his Perestroika, and what were the results?

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:32.450
Terrible.

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:33.450
Perestroika liberalized overnight a Soviet
economy that was planned from the beginning,

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:34.450
it was a devastating blow.

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:35.450
First, government officials approved the creation
of "free" prices, no longer controlled and

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:36.450
set by the state.

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:37.450
Only 4% of the citizens approved this maneuver.

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:38.450
The result?

00:33:38.450 --> 00:33:39.450
A race to the top of basic necessities and
a hyperinflation that in 1992 would have touched

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:40.450
peaks of Two Thousand Six Hundred %. Money
was rationed and in its place a sort of "consumer

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:41.450
card" was introduced, they were sheets of
paper worth various amounts of rubles, with

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:42.450
which citizens could make purchases without
using cash money.

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:43.450
When they found a piece of bread after hours
standing in line, Russians could count themselves

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:44.450
lucky if they could buy a half-pound loaf
of bread for 6 dollars, compared to an average

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:45.450
monthly salary of fifty dollars.

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:46.450
Although the Soviet standard of living until
then had always been at least a third lower

00:33:46.450 --> 00:33:47.450
than that of Westerners, citizens noticed
the difference with respect to previous times:

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:48.450
crime, especially the mafia, was lower and
there was a general sense of greater security.

00:33:48.450 --> 00:33:49.450
Whether this was real or not is another story.

00:33:49.450 --> 00:33:50.450
With Perestroika there was a free-for-all
for those who wanted to do business with large

00:33:50.450 --> 00:33:51.450
foreign multinationals, an incentive from
which stemmed a whole series of speculation

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:52.450
and fake sales, like the ones of the strategic
Soviet mining companies at symbolic prices.

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:53.450
On top of that, there was the Issuance of
new currencies for all the republics of the

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:54.450
Union.

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:55.450
This increased the confusion of internal transactions
and trade.

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:56.450
In addition, the so-called "property law"
of 1990, which removed the state from ownership

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:57.450
of key industries, generated 2 million unemployed
nationwide, a figure never seen before.

00:33:57.450 --> 00:33:58.450
And as we know, an unemployed population use
more and more alcohol and drugs, to say the

00:33:58.450 --> 00:33:59.450
least.

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:00.450
By 98, the Russian economy, in the hands of
mobsters and foreign entrepreneurs, would

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:01.450
have been almost completely halved from 1990
figures, going from a GDP of five hundred

00:34:01.450 --> 00:34:02.450
and ten billion to a GDP of two hundred and
seventy.

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:03.450
Food prices had doubled, and wages were less
than half.

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:04.450
While, typhoid, cholera and other diseases
had reached epidemic proportions in the country.

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:05.450
It was as if the entire nation had suddenly
gone back 80 years.

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:06.450
Some, such as historian Stephen Cohen, have
called this achievement the result of a particular

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:07.450
phenomenon, Gangster Capitalism.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:08.450
The opening up to international markets also
encouraged the arrival of private banks, which

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:09.450
replaced the monopoly of the State Bank.

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:10.450
Soviet debt to Western banks jumped from twenty
seven point two billion dollars in 1985 to

00:34:10.450 --> 00:34:11.450
forty billion $ in 1986.

00:34:11.450 --> 00:34:12.450
Wanting to reform things too quickly also
invested the political front.

00:34:12.450 --> 00:34:13.450
Gorbachev knew that the Party - and its members
- were the operational core.

00:34:13.450 --> 00:34:14.450
If he wanted to change the Union structure,
the only way was to strip Party members of

00:34:14.450 --> 00:34:15.450
their various Departments and deprive them
of their powers.

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:16.450
With a series of reforms Gorbachev did just
that, he took away the Party's decision-making

00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:17.450
monopoly.

00:34:17.450 --> 00:34:18.450
In doing so, however, it was like beheading
the State, it was a political suicide, in

00:34:18.450 --> 00:34:19.450
all respects.

00:34:19.450 --> 00:34:20.450
Without a head - the State - the body (the
Soviet Union) suddenly stopped working, a

00:34:20.450 --> 00:34:21.450
bit like unplugging a device.

00:34:21.450 --> 00:34:22.450
At that point, all it took was a little push,
an international event of great impact that

00:34:22.450 --> 00:34:23.450
could maybe break up the entire Eastern bloc.

00:34:23.450 --> 00:34:24.450
Oh, yeah, how silly, this already happened
in 1989: --- With the symbolic fall of the

00:34:24.450 --> 00:34:25.450
Berlin Wall, like a domino, all the Soviet
republics, starting with the Baltic ones,

00:34:25.450 --> 00:34:26.450
broke away one after the other in the wake
of the independence euphoria that horrified

00:34:26.450 --> 00:34:27.450
the Russian nationalists.

00:34:27.450 --> 00:34:28.450
And nationalism was the final card, the ace
of spades that would close the game.

00:34:28.450 --> 00:34:29.450
The first to realize that the rules of that
game were changing, was Boris Yeltsin.

00:34:29.450 --> 00:34:30.450
Like an old fox, Yeltsin exploited the frustrations
of the most radical communists and some of

00:34:30.450 --> 00:34:31.450
the members of the secret service to break
away from Gorbachev's wing, dress up as a

00:34:31.450 --> 00:34:32.450
populist leader, go into opposition, and support
a coup against Gorbachev in 1991 in the capital

00:34:32.450 --> 00:34:33.450
Moscow.

00:34:33.450 --> 00:34:34.450
The images of him getting on the tank would
go around the world, angering the most dissatisfied

00:34:34.450 --> 00:34:35.450
people who were fed up with Gorbachev's policies.

00:34:35.450 --> 00:34:36.450
The coup failed immediately, because obviously
there was no longer a state.

00:34:36.450 --> 00:34:37.450
It must be said that 70% of the population
wanted to maintain the Soviet Union, the referendum

00:34:37.450 --> 00:34:38.450
on the preservation of the USSR in 1991 clearly
demonstrated this.

00:34:38.450 --> 00:34:39.450
But these were not Yeltsin's plans.

00:34:39.450 --> 00:34:40.450
Yeltsin had all the popular support to be
able to obscure Gorbachev, take away his remaining

00:34:40.450 --> 00:34:41.450
consensus and force him to resign at the end
of August.

00:34:41.450 --> 00:34:42.450
In November of the same year Yeltsin would
issue a decree with which to make illegal

00:34:42.450 --> 00:34:43.450
any activity of the Communist Party and dissolve
once and for all the Soviet State.

00:34:43.450 --> 00:34:44.450
On December 11, 1991 only two of the 15 Soviet
republics were still part of the Union: Russia

00:34:44.450 --> 00:34:45.450
and Kazakhstan.

00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:46.450
The former left the Union the following day,
on December 12.

00:34:46.450 --> 00:34:47.450
That was the end of the USSR and the beginning
of the Russian Federation.

00:34:47.450 --> 00:34:48.450
[gentlemen titanic].

00:34:48.450 --> 00:34:49.450
It’s not over yet.

00:34:49.450 --> 00:34:50.450
With the exit of Russia, Kazakhstan became
the Soviet Union.

00:34:50.450 --> 00:34:51.450
Yep.

00:34:51.450 --> 00:34:52.450
For 4 days, until it declared itself an independent
republic on December 16, 1991, Kazakhstan

00:34:52.450 --> 00:34:53.450
was basically the Soviet Union.

00:34:53.450 --> 00:34:54.450
Oh great Kazakhstan, thank you, you created
a nightmare that even Lenin would never have

00:34:54.450 --> 00:34:55.450
dared to dream.

00:34:55.450 --> 00:34:56.450
I've been wanting to make this video for almost
a year, I tried 3 times previously but I always

00:34:56.450 --> 00:34:57.450
gave up.

00:34:57.450 --> 00:34:58.450
Summarizing the reasons for the collapse of
the Soviet Union in a 30 minutes video was

00:34:58.450 --> 00:34:59.450
unthinkable.

00:34:59.450 --> 00:35:00.450
Yet here we are.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:01.450
Thanks to all of you for listening to me until
the end.

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:02.450
See you in the next episode.

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:02.451
Ciao!

